Fabric shared coordination and outage behavior
==============================================

Automatic Fabric Connections require a stable event ID and an affirmative
Redis/Valkey SET NX response before a chain can run. A configured cooldown also
requires an affirmative shared claim. The coordinator refuses missing identities,
unavailable clients, lookup errors, timeouts and malformed replies. It does not
replace shared claims with worker-local memory during an outage.

Client construction failures are retried on a later event. Commands have bounded
connect/read timeouts and do not automatically retry uncertain writes. A failed
or lost reply does not release a claim: the server may have already committed it.
A healthy duplicate/cooldown denial remains a normal skip. Refusal warnings do not
include raw connection exceptions or event payloads.

Compatibility and limits
------------------------

Existing key names are retained, including the connection/event claim's 300-second
TTL and the configured cooldown TTL. Coordinating through the same healthy store
suppresses duplicate attempts within that lifetime. All event workers must be
upgraded before relying on the outage behavior; an older worker can still fail
open. No persistent exactly-once, automatic retry/delivery, partition fencing,
rollback of an external effect, or guarantee across lost broker state is added.

Operator-initiated run_once remains an explicit test path. It does not claim a
bus event, and still applies the normal author, permission, scope and staging
checks. The runtime's cached Connection freshness and best-effort audit recording
remain separate concerns.

On a coordination outage, inspect the fixed Fabric refusal warnings and broker
health. Restore the configured shared store and verify a new safe event before
re-enabling operational assumptions. Missed events are not replayed automatically;
review any uncertain external effect before requesting a manual retry.
